cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: >-
  Measuring the Gap Shape of Sum-of-Squares Relaxations of Unique Games:
  A Certified Null Result, a Completeness Filter, and the Structure of the
  Degree-4 Extension on Paley Graphs
version: 1.0.0
date-released: "2026-09-18"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/big-brain-zaru/games-conjecture"
authors:
  - family-names: Zaru
    given-names: "Nadim F."
    affiliation: "Independent Researcher"
abstract: >-
  A certified computational study of one scalar derived from a stated open
  question about Khot's Unique Games Conjecture. The gap shape of an instance is
  C_d = (1 - opt) / (1 - R_d) for a degree-d relaxation; because diluting an
  instance with a perfectly satisfiable part leaves it unchanged, the
  Khot-Moshkovitz question of whether a (1-eps, 1-C eps) degree-d Lasserre gap
  exists with C unbounded is exactly whether the supremum of C_d is infinite.

  The headline is a null result, and the plan written before the search predicted
  it. Across every family searched the largest certified degree-4 gap shape is
  1.093586, and degree 4 is exact or nearly exact nearly everywhere.

  The positive content is narrow and concerns the Paley graphs, where the
  degree-2 gap survives degree 4 exactly from p = 29. The question reduces to a
  single finite feasibility problem. No extension exists at p = 13 or 17, proved
  by exact integer dual certificates with no floating point in the verification.
  Extensions exist at p = 29, 37, 41, 53 and 61, always exactly on the boundary;
  the moment matrix has rank (p-1)(p-7)/8 at all five, and the upper bound of
  that formula is proved with the kernel identified explicitly. The extension is
  bilinear, hence one positive semidefinite form correcting the Wick lift, and at
  p = 29, where it is unique, it lives in Q(sqrt p, zeta_{(p-1)/4}) rather than
  Q(sqrt p).

  Every reported value comes from a direct evaluation, a machine-checkable
  certificate, or an exhaustive enumeration, and the whole record recomputes with
  132 checks and 0 mismatches. Five corrections are kept in place, including two
  findings demoted after a literature check and one prediction that was
  pre-registered, confirmed, and then refuted. No progress is made on the
  conjecture itself.
keywords:
  - unique games conjecture
  - sum-of-squares
  - Lasserre hierarchy
  - semidefinite programming
  - integrality gap
  - Paley graph
  - Max-Cut
  - Cayley graph
  - pseudo-expectation
  - certified computation
  - reproducible research
  - negative result
